                0.372
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 13.10"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.387,
            0.881,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": "If we denote the points A(–4, 5) and B (–1, 2) as $( x _ { 1 } , y _ { 1 } )$ and $( x _ { \\mathrm { { } _ { 2 } } } , y _ { \\mathrm { { } _ { 2 } } } )$ respectively, then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.327,
            0.428,
            0.668,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Suppose} = \\frac{\\mathrm{AP}}{\\mathrm{BP}} = \\frac{2 - 5}{- 1 - ( - 4)} = \\frac{- 3}{3} = - 1.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.468,
            0.878,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": "$(x_{1},y_{1})$ and A(–4, 5) as $( x _ { _ 2 } , y _ { _ 2 } )$ then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.323,
            0.508,
            0.672,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Slope} = \\frac{\\mathrm{PA}}{\\mathrm{PB}} = \\frac{5 - 2}{- 4 - ( - 1)} = \\frac{3}{- 3} = - 1.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.55,
            0.881,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "Hence the slope remains the same irrespective of the order in which we choose the points."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.594,
            0.884,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": "In general, if $(x_{1},y_{1})$ and $(x_{2},y_{2})$ are two points on a line, the slope is given by the expression $\\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}$ and it remains the same for any two points on the line. There is a simple and elegant proof of this result which you will see below."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.737,
            0.467,
            0.759
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "13.3.2 Slope-Intercept Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.768,
            0.884,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": "In the chapter on linear polynomials, we were introduced to the fact that in the linear equation $y = mx + d, \\text{\" } m \\text{\" }$ represents the slope or steepness of the line while ‘d’ represents the y-intercept, that is, the length cut off by the line on the y-axis from the origin. Thus, y = mx + d is also referred to as the **slope-intercept form of a line**."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
